---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Topbar from "../../../components/Topbar.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import { site } from "../../../lib/site";
const w = site.writing[1];
---
<Base page="writing" title={`${site.name} — ${w.title_en}`}>
  <Topbar path="/writing/" />
  <main>
    <section class="pad page">
      <div class="sec-head sf">
        <a href="/writing/" class="caption" style="color:var(--soft)">← Writing</a>
        <span class="kicker" style="margin-top:18px; display:inline-flex">{w.kind}<span class="kicker-zh">{w.kind_zh || w.kind}</span></span>
        <h1 class="sec-title" style="margin-top:14px">{w.title_en}<span class="sec-title-zh">{w.title_zh}</span></h1>
        <div class="article-meta"><span>{w.year}</span><span>{w.venue_en}</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="article sf">
        <p class="article-lede">{w.detail_en}</p>
        <p class="article-lede zh">{w.detail_zh}</p>
        <div class="article-body">
          <p>Digital technology substantially altered our media consumption by enabling multiple modes to produce, package and organise content. The transformation of material across media forms has become more accessible through computers and AI generation tools. Consequently, individuals can remix and adapt media with greater facility, thereby blurring the boundaries between consumers and producers (Papacharissi, 2011).</p>
          <p>This project represents a participation in media creation that explores the possibility and effects of transforming media within these contexts. The original artefact, created for week 3, transformed a cut-up version of the script from the first scene of The Brutalist into voice using a text-to-speech service. AIgenerated video and music from the same scene were subsequently added to provide a multimedia experience.</p>
          <p>The large media project applies enhanced operations to five films, expanding and consolidating the concept of transformation while also exploring adaptation, remediation and convergence of media. Rather than displaying all works linearly as in the original artefact, the large media project provides separate voice, music and video files in the folder of each film. Scripts for voice are cut-ups of screenplays automatically produced on chardin.neocities.org/cut_up.</p>
          <p>These are then processed through the text-to-speech service of luvvoice.com to be transformed into audio. Music is generated by Suno. The prompts given are generated by Gemini through its interpretation of the description of each film on IMDb.</p>
          <p>Video clips are generated by the AI service Doubao, while the prompts derive from original descriptions in screenplays. The selected films are A Real Pain, All We Imagine as Light, Challengers, Conclave and The Brutalist. These films address considerably varied themes and unfold in different contexts and environments.</p>
          <p>The production selects iconic scenes to emphasise their distinctive styles. The portfolio thus presents diverse contents within the same structure, extending the practice to multiple contents and genres. Screenplays possess distinctive characteristics that qualify them as the original material.</p>
          <p>Pasolini (1986) describes a film script as “a structure that wants to be another structure” in the title of his book, arguing that it conveys not only written language but also the potential film. This project extends the products from films to collections of various media. Producing media from screenplays constitutes a parallel step with making films, which could also be viewed as an adaptation of the film.</p>
          <p>According to Hutcheon and O’Flynn (2013, p. 7), “an adaptation is an announced and extensive transposition of a particular work or works.” They propose that either the medium or the genre may be shifted, while this practice alters the former. During the transformation from screenplays to collections of media, changes of frame convert the context and thereby “create a manifestly different interpretation” independent of the original film.</p>
          <p>The multimedia product interprets the scene through voice, music and video simultaneously, thereby conveying significantly richer and more diverse information than script or film alone. Beyond typical adaptation, the project attempts to depict a complete representation of the scene by incorporating more information than the film within the collection. Organising different mediums into a single collection stimulates convergence among media forms.</p>
          <p>Combining various media forms within the same object creates multimodality or multimediality, allowing information from one medium to resonate with others in the collection through intermediality (Ryan &amp; Thon, 2014). To fully comprehend the artwork, audiences must synthesise information across independent and fragmented media segments, which is the occasion when convergence occurs, as Jenkins (2006) argues. Each medium in the collection makes a “distinctive and valuable contribution” (Jenkins, 2006, pp. 95-96) to the eventual representation of the scene by employing “its specifics and possibilities” (Stam, 2006, p. 11).</p>
          <p>Additionally, convergence continues within the minds of audiences when they interpret the media individually. Consumers construct their “own personal mythology from bits and fragments of information” (Jenkins, 2006, p. 3) from separate media works in the collection. The subjectivity of human perception generates varied understandings of the same collection among individuals, triggering their unique modes of interpretation.</p>
          <p>Furthermore, the selected media in this project provide only limited and selective presentations in comparison with films. For instance, the voice of cut-up texts contains no coherent narrative, while the 5- second video clips convey substantially less than film clips lasting several minutes. Moreover, the design of the interactive collection structure, which enables audiences to choose the order and method of viewing, results in a negotiated decoding process (Shaw, 2017).</p>
          <p>These factors collectively lead audiences to organise and complete the expression independently, thereby increasing their participation in the process of convergence. Ryan and Thon (2014, p. 3) articulate this phenomenon as “it is the reader, spectator, listener, or player who uses the blueprint of a finished text to construct a mental image of this world.” Considering the specific content within each piece of media, the role of computer generation cannot be overlooked. This project employs both traditional text-to-speech generation and AI</p>
          <p>music and video creation. Automation circumvents the subjectivity of humans in filmmaking, attempting to reveal meanings of the scripts more directly. The interface “erases itself” (Bolter &amp; Grusin, 2002, p. 23), enabling users to interact more intimately with the content through transparency.</p>
          <p>AI employs machine learning that contains less human subjectivity, avoiding the direct algorithms designed by programmers in previous computer creations. Human intervention was further reduced by providing only original film script texts in prompts. The project seeks to offer more intimate interpretations of the scene to achieve immediacy.</p>
          <p>The combination of different media within a single project corresponds to another concept Bolter and Grusin discuss in their work, namely hypermediacy. Separate components in the collection can be received in different sequences or formats. Cotten and Oliver (1993, p. 8) described this structure as “a medium that offers ‘random access’; it has no physical beginning, middle, or end.” Audiences are afforded the freedom to access this media collection in their preferred manner, which foregrounds the medium’s presence.</p>
          <p>Different media provide diversified experiences in this project based on their unique characteristics, fulfilling distinct functions in the collection. Hypermedia “reproduce the rich sensorium of human experience” (Bolter &amp; Grusin, 2002, p. 34) when audiences integrate multiple media during their comprehension. Digital technologies enable us to create and organise content in novel ways, achieving immediacy and hypermediacy simultaneously within this collection.</p>
          <p>The coexistence of these two logics generates a more intense and dynamic outcome when hypermediacy is “both acknowledging and undercutting the desire for immediacy” (Bolter &amp; Grusin, 2002, p. 34). This effectively transforms and diversifies the audience’s experience, thereby achieving remediation, which is defined by Bolter and Grusin (2002, p. 45) as “representation of one medium in another”. Viewers of the media collections may compare them with the original film and experience the interplay between them (Bolter &amp; Grusin, 2002), which enables them to engage deeply with the production.</p>
          <p>The project interprets film scenes through the structure of multimedia collections, encompassing voice, music and videos. Extended from screenplays, it creates an interactive and negotiated decoding experience for each audience to conceptualise the scene independently. Simultaneously, as adaptations and remediations of the films, the project explores parallel routes to convey messages more immediately and effectively.</p>
          <p>Digital technologies offer opportunities to transform and converge media across multiple mediums, thereby expanding the possibilities of original artworks. We consequently participate in media production and reproduction, transcending the singular consumer identity.</p>
        </div>
          <div class="article-refs">
            <h2>References</h2>
            <ol>
            <li>Bolter, J. D., &amp; Grusin, R. (2002). Remediation: Understanding new media (5th pr.). MIT Press.</li>
            <li>Cotten, B., &amp; Oliver, R. (1993). Understanding hypermedia. Phaidon Press.</li>
            <li>Hutcheon, L., &amp; O’Flynn, S. (2013). A theory of adaptation (2nd ed.). Routledge.</li>
            <li>Jenkins, H. (2006). Convergence culture: Where old and new media collide. New York University Press.</li>
            <li>Papacharissi, Z. A. (2011). A networked self: Identity, community, and culture on social network sites. Routledge.</li>
            <li>Pasolini, P. P. (1986). The screenplay as a “structure that wants to be another structure.” The American Journal of Semiotics, 4(1), 53–72. https://doi.org/10.5840/ajs198641/28</li>
            <li>Ryan, M.-L., &amp; Thon, J.-N. (Eds.). (2014). Storyworlds across media: Toward a media-conscious narratology. University of Nebraska Press. https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctt1d9nkdg</li>
            <li>Shaw, A. (2017). Encoding and decoding affordances: Stuart Hall and interactive media technologies. Media, Culture &amp; Society, 39(4), 592–602. https://doi.org/10.1177/0163443717692741</li>
            <li>Stam, R. (2006). François Truffaut and friends: Modernism, sexuality, and film adaptation. Rutgers University Press. https://doi.org/10.36019/9780813540993</li>
            </ol>
          </div>      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <SiteFooter />
</Base>
